#. §0: `contrapose!`
#. §1: `⊢ P → Q`
#. §2: `⊢ ¬ Q → ¬ P`
#. §3: `push_neg`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.pf_docs
msgid "The §0 tacic turns a goal of the form §1 into §2 and pushes negations through (with §3)."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `=`
#. §1: `A B`
#. §2: `x : u`
#. §3: `x ∈ A`
#. §4: `x ∈ B`
#. §5: ```
#. set_eq_iff
#.   (u : Type*}
#.   (A B : Set u)
#.     A = B ↔ ∀ x, x ∈ A ↔ x ∈ B
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.set_docs
msgid "This helps to define the §0 relation between sets. We say that two sets §1 are equal to mean that for every §2, §3 if and only if §4. Here's what it looks like in Lean:\n"
"§5"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `obtain`
#. §1: `obtain`
#. §2: `obtain`
#. §3: `exact`
#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.L02_arg
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip**\n"
"The §0 tactic is *very* handy especially when you'll need to use the assumption introduced by §1 in more intricate ways. But if you find yourself using §2 to introduce a hypothesis to only then call the tactic §3 on that hypothesis, then you probably could have made your work a little shorter."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L04_equiv
msgid "Great, we're almost home. Try to to finish this level off on your own."
msgstr ""

#: Game
msgid "This game is designed to accompany students through MA276."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `P → R`
#. §1: `P`
#. §2: `P → Q`
#. §3: `intro`
#. §4: `intro`
#. §5: `intro`
#. §6: ```
#. intro hP
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.L06_arg
msgid "We wish to prove that §0 -- our goal, itself, is an implication. We know that implications are true by default if the hypothesis is false. So we might as well assume that §1 is true (otherwise §2 is definitely true). How do we do this in Lean?\n"
"### **❯ The §3 tactic**\n"
"To *introduce* the hypothesis of an implication as a goal, we use the §4 tactic. 🔍 Check out the entry for §5 in the **Tactics** tab. 👉 In our specific situation, try:\n"
"§6\n"
""
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L02_equiv
msgid "Contrapositives"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L02_equiv
msgid "# **Level 2: Contrapositives**\n"
"Let's use the laws of logic to prove that the ***Contrapositive*** of an implication is logically equivalent to the original implication."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `by_contra!`
#. §1: `by_contra`
#. §2: `push_neg`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.pf_docs
msgid "The tactic §0 is the same as §1 except that it applies §2 automically to the introduced hypothesis."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `∨`
#. §1: $$ (P ∨ Q) ↔ (Q ∨ P) $$
#. §2: `Or_comm`
#. §3: $$
#. \\begin{array}{|cc||cc|}
#.   \\hline
#.   P & Q & P ∨ Q & Q ∨ P  \\
#.   \\hline
#.   T & T & T & T \\
#.   T & F & T & T \\
#.   F & T & T & T \\
#.   F & F & F & F \\
#.   \\hline
#. \\end{array}
#. $$
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.equiv_docs
msgid "This is the **Commutative Law** for §0. It says that\n"
"§1\n"
"The proof of §2 follows from the truth-table below:\n"
"§3"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.ArgWorld_eg.L04_arg
#: Game.Levels.QuantifierWorld.QuantifierWorld_eg.L04_quant
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L04_pf
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.PfWorld_eg.L04_pf
msgid "Example 4"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.L07_arg
msgid "Proving a disjunction: Part 1"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `P`
#. §1: `¬ Q`
#. §2: `R`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.L02_equiv
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip**\n"
"The result of this level tells you that if you want to prove and if-then statement where the conclusion is a disjunction, then you may do so by... **assuming** that §0 and §1 are true, and then showing that §2 must follow."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `A B : Set u`
#. §1: `∪`
#. §2: ```
#. A ∩ B = {x | x ∈ A ∨ x ∈ B}
#. ```
#. §3: `x ∈ A ∪ B`
#. §4: `x ∈ A v x ∈ B`
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L04_set
msgid "## **Level 4**\n"
"If §0 then the set connective §1 (union) is defined as follows:\n"
"§2\n"
"This means that, definitionally, to say that §3 means the same thing as §4."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.SetWorld
msgid "Set World"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `by_contra`
#. §1: `⊢ P`
#. §2: `⊢ False`
#. §3: `this : ¬ P`
#. §4: `this`
#. §5: `by_contra F`
#. §6: `⊢ False`
#. §7: `F : ¬ P`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.pf_docs
msgid "The tactic §0 changes a goal of the form §1 to §2 and adds the hypothesis §3 to the proof-state. One can name the introduced hypothesis §4 with an optional identifier. For example, §5 changes the goal to §6 and adds the hypothesis §7."
msgstr ""

